# What happens when prices are not allowed to move?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Queues, black markets, rationing: what replaces the price? What did empty Soviet shelves and wartime ration books have in common?"
next: {"title":"Why would a market ever be efficient?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/22-why-would-a-market-ever-be-efficient.cast"}

## Two bare shelves
sources:
    - id: wiki_uk
      title: Rationing in the United Kingdom
      authors: Wikipedia
      finding: Bread was not rationed during the Second World War; it was rationed from 21 July 1946 to 24 July 1948.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rationing_in_the_United_Kingdom
    - id: bodleian1954
      title: 'On this day in 1954: Food rationing ends after 14 years'
      authors: Bodleian Libraries, University of Oxford
      year: 2011
      finding: Rationing began in the UK on 8 January 1940 with limits on butter, bacon and sugar; the limits ended at midnight on 3 July 1954, meat being the last food freed.
      url: https://blogs.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/archivesandmanuscripts/2011/07/04/on-this-day-in-1954-food-rationing-ends-after-14-years/
    - id: kornai1980
      title: Economics of Shortage
      authors: János Kornai
      year: 1980
      finding: 'Chronic shortage in the socialist economies of Central and Eastern Europe was systemic: queues for basic goods, waiting lists for durables, forced substitution and forced saving.'
    - id: csm1991
      title: Prices Take a Hike in Soviet Capital
      authors: The Christian Science Monitor
      year: 1991
      finding: Soviet state price rises took effect on 2 April 1991; some food items, such as meat and bread, tripled in price.
      url: https://www.csmonitor.com/1991/0403/03031.html

    icon book cites ["bodleian1954"] size 280 set twemoji x 250 y 440 or ["closed book","notebook"] icon_look picture of ledger hidden true
    text book_l "Britain" x 250 y 268 font_size 36 hidden true
    text book_cue "8 Jan 1940" #b5482e cites ["bodleian1954"] x 250 y 228 font_size 34 hidden true
    icon bacon size 70 set twemoji x 440 y 520 icon_look picture of bacon hidden true
    icon butter size 70 set twemoji x 440 y 430 icon_look picture of butter hidden true
    icon meat size 70 set twemoji x 440 y 340 or ["cut of meat"] icon_look picture of "meat on bone" hidden true
    scale ends bottom cites ["bodleian1954"] label "Food rationing ends" unit "" min 1944 max 1960 value 1954 ticks 8 hidden true
    path bread_tick #b5482e points [[200,118],[200,165]] hidden true
    text bread "bread rationed" #b5482e cites ["wiki_uk"] x 200 y 190 font_size 30 hidden true
    path shelf_frame #3d3833 points [[590,370],[590,580],[890,580],[890,370],[590,370]] hidden true
    path plank1 points [[590,440],[890,440]] hidden true
    path plank2 points [[590,510],[890,510]] hidden true
    icon loaf size 56 set twemoji x 650 y 475 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    group shelf members ["shelf_frame","plank1","plank2","loaf"] hidden true
    icon p1 size 100 set twemoji x 610 y 290 or ["standing person"] icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon p2 size 100 set twemoji x 700 y 290 or ["standing person"] icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon p3 size 100 set twemoji x 790 y 290 or ["standing person"] icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon p4 size 100 set twemoji x 880 y 290 or ["standing person"] icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    group queue members ["p1","p2","p3","p4"] hidden true
    text shelf_l "USSR, 1980s" cites ["kornai1980"] x 740 y 205 font_size 36 hidden true
    text triple "2 Apr 1991: meat, bread ×3" #b5482e cites ["csm1991"] x 740 y 610 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Two bare shelves"

What happens when a price is not allowed to rise, however much people want the thing? Start wi (@draw book_l@)th a little book.
    draw book

On the eighth of January 1940, Britain began rationin (@draw bacon@)g food: baco (@draw butter@)n, butter and sugar firs (@draw meat@)t, then meat, then much more.
    draw book_cue

B: With a war on, that makes sense. Ships were being sunk; there simply wasn't enough food.
    highlight book_cue effect underline

True. Now the odd part. The war in Europe ended in May 1945. When did Britain stop rationing food?
    draw ends

    ask "Click the year you think food rationing ended in Britain." on ends store g
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true}. Most people guess much earlier.

July 1954: nine years after the war. And bread, never rationed during the war, was rationed from 1946.
    draw bread_tick bread

B: So the shortage outlived the war. Something else kept the shelves bare.
    erase ends bread bread_tick

Now a second picture: a Soviet shop in the 1980s, with a long queue and half-empty shelves.
    draw shelf shelf_l

B: That was peacetime, though. No submarines.
    draw queue

No. But the state set the prices of bread and meat, and held them low. When it finally raised them, on the second of Ap (@draw triple@)ril 1991, meat and bread roughly tripled overnight.

B: So in both places the price was held down, and people wanted far more than there was.
    text held "price held down" #b5482e x 500 y 135 font_size 46

That is what they share. And when the price may not do its job, the job doesn't vanish: something else decides who gets the bread.
    highlight book shelf effect pulse

## Who collects the gap?
vars: {"w":{"value":16,"min":4,"max":48},"qmin":{"expr":"240/w"}}
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a week (Q)
    y_label: Price a loaf (P)
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: $5
      q_label: ''
    price_ceiling:
      level: 30
      label: Cap $3
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 120
      w: 620
      h: 540

    path gaprect closed #c9a23a points [[0,30],[26.6,30],[26.6,70],[0,70]] data true style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.55 hidden true
    path pd_line dashed #b5482e points [[0,70],[26.6,70]] data true hidden true
    text pd_l "worth $7" #b5482e at.data [13,63] font_size 28 hidden true
    text gap_l "gap $4" at.data [13,40] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch who work ["Who collects it?","Queue: time","Black market: cash","Coupons: rules"] x 812 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch qread work ["Time: ${w:0} an hour","Queue: {qmin:0} min"] x 812 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Who collects the gap?"

B: To see what that something is, take Harbourtown in a hard winter, with illustrative numbers: bread at Mia's bakery.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

B: Left alone, the price settles at five dollars a loaf.
    draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar

Now the harbour council caps it at thre (@fade guide_lines label_Pstar to 0.35@)e dollars.
    draw ceiling_line label_ceiling

B: At three dollars, more people want bread  (@draw shortage_guide_qs label_Qs@)and Mia bakes less of it. That ga (@draw shortage_arrow label_shortage@)p is the shortage.
    draw shortage_guide_qd label_Qd

So who gets the loaves that are baked?
    highlight shortage_guide_qs label_Qs

B: Start with a better question: what would the last buyer gladly pay for them? Read it off the demand cu (@draw pd_l@)rve: about seven dollars.
    draw pd_line

So each loaf carries a four-dollar gap between what it's worth to the buyer and what the till says. Someone collects it.
    draw gaprect gap_l

B: In a queue, buyers pay it in time. The line grows until the wait costs about four dollars, and that time is simply lost.
    draw who_box who_line_1 who_line_2

And on a black market, sellers sneak the price back up to seven and pocket the gap.
    draw who_line_3

B: With ration books, the state hands out the right to buy, and the gap goes to whoever holds a coupon.
    draw who_line_4

So Soviet queues and British coupons were two answers to one question: who gets a loaf, once the price may not decide.
    highlight gaprect effect box

    erase who

    draw qread_box qread_line_1 qread_line_2

B: Try it yourself: change what an hour of waiting is worth, and watch how long the queue for each four-dollar gap must grow.
    explore params ["w"]

## But who needs it most?
sources:
    - id: weitzman1977
      title: Is the Price System or Rationing More Effective in Getting a Commodity to Those Who Need It Most?
      authors: Martin L. Weitzman
      year: 1977
      finding: 'Bell Journal of Economics 8(2): 517-524. The price system does relatively better when wants are more dispersed or income more evenly distributed; rationing does relatively better when needs are more uniform or income inequality greater.'
      url: https://ideas.repec.org/a/rje/bellje/v8y1977iautumnp517-524.html

    text clean "Clean answer: let P move" #8f887c x 500 y 640 font_size 32 hidden true
    text recap "time · cash · coupons" x 500 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    text rich "$7 bread → the well-off first?" #b5482e x 500 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    text weitzman "Martin Weitzman, 1977" #8a5fa8 cites ["weitzman1977"] x 500 y 600 font_size 34 hidden true
    text h_price "Prices win" #2f6b8f x 290 y 550 font_size 40 hidden true
    text h_ration "Rations win" #b5482e x 710 y 550 font_size 40 hidden true
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[500,420],[500,575]] hidden true
    text p1 "needs differ" cites ["weitzman1977"] x 290 y 495 font_size 34 hidden true
    text p2 "incomes similar" cites ["weitzman1977"] x 290 y 452 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r1 "needs alike" cites ["weitzman1977"] x 710 y 495 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r2 "incomes unequal" cites ["weitzman1977"] x 710 y 452 font_size 34 hidden true
    icon car size 80 set twemoji x 130 y 550 or ["car"] icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    icon loaf size 80 set twemoji x 870 y 550 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    text s1 "Something always rations: consensus" #4f7a45 x 500 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s2 "Coupons or prices? contested" #b5482e x 500 y 255 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch wq work ["wait = gap ÷ value of time","$4 ÷ $16 an hour","= ¼ hour = 15 minutes"] x 500 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "But who needs it most?"

So the price never really vanishes: it's paid in ti (@draw recap@)me, in cash under the counter, or in coupons. Sounds like a clean case for letting prices move.
    draw clean

B: Not so fas (@fade clean to 0.4@)t. In 1940, bread at seven dollars wou (@draw rich@)ld have fed the well-off first, not the hungry.
    erase recap

That's a serious objec (@draw weitzman divider h_price h_ration@)tion, and the economist Martin Weitzman made it precise in 1977.
    erase rich

B: His answer: prices match goods to needs better when needs differ a lot and  (@draw p2@)incomes are fairly equal.
    draw p1

And rationing does better when everyone needs about the same and inc (@draw r2@)omes are very unequal.
    draw r1

B: Food in wartime fits that s (@erase weitzman@)econd case: everyone needs calories, and wages differed hugely.
    draw loaf

While a car fits the first: some need one badly, some hardly at all, and a waiting list can't tell them apart.
    draw car

B: So how sure are we? That something always  (@draw s1@)takes the price's place: that's consensus.

Whether coupons beat prices in a given crisis depends on needs (@draw s2@) and incomes, and that is still contested.

    fade h_price h_ration divider p1 p2 r1 r2 car loaf s1 s2 clean to 0.1

    draw mathq

    ask "How long is the queue for a loaf? Open the math, or skip it." on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

    fade h_price h_ration divider p1 p2 r1 r2 car loaf s1 s2 clean to 0.12

B: How long is a queue? Divide the gap by what an hour is worth to the people in it.
    draw wq_box wq_line_1

B: Say a four-dollar gap, and time worth sixteen dollars an hour: about fifteen (@draw wq_line_3@) minutes in line for every loaf.
    draw wq_line_2

    erase wq

@test

    erase mathq

    fade h_price h_ration divider p1 p2 r1 r2 car loaf s1 s2 to 1

    fade clean to 0.4

    quiz "A concert sells tickets at $50, though fans would pay $300, and resale is banned. What decides who gets in?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: Waiting, luck and touts: the $250 gap is still collected, just not by the box office.
        wrong: If the price can't rise to $300, something else must sort the fans: here, waiting, luck and touts.
        * Nothing: fans just get a bargain
        + Waiting online, luck and touts
        * The band's costs

    quiz "A city makes downtown parking free. Where does the gap go?"
        right: Into circling: drivers pay for the scarce spaces with their time, a queue on wheels.
        wrong: There are still more cars than spaces: here, the gap goes into time spent circling.
        * To drivers, as savings
        + Into time spent circling for a space
        * To the city

    quiz "After a flood, every household gets a water coupon, and coupons may be sold. Compared with one long queue, what changes?" voice b
        right: Coupons can move to those who need water most, the sellers keep the gap as cash, and nobody wastes hours in line.
        wrong: Ask what a household that needs little water does with its coupon: here, it sells it to someone who needs more, and nobody queues.
        * Nothing: same water, same people
        + Those who need less sell to those who need more, and no time is lost queuing
        * Only the rich get water

When a price may not move, the shortage is still shared out, by time, by cash or by rule. The real choice is which of those we want, and who should get the bread.
    highlight h_price h_ration effect pulse

    pause 0.5

